<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fuvahmulah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fuvahmulah is a single-island atoll in the far south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, between Huvadhu and Addu atolls, unusual for its freshwater lakes and the absence of a lagoon. In 1959 it joined Addu and Huvadhu in the breakaway United Suvadive Republic, which the Maldivian government suppressed by 1963. It is now one of the country's cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fuvahmulah.jpg" alt="Fuvahmulah" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GIS &amp; Mapping Unit, Spatial Planning Section, Department of National Planning, Republic of Maldives · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuvahmulah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fuvahmulah lies 494.2 km south of the capital, Malé, and is the third largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, about 4.5 by 1.2 km, with a submerged reef, Rashikedefaro, running some 3 km to the south-east. It was once a small coral atoll whose southern end lay open at Diyarehifaando, enclosing a saltwater lagoon that formed a natural harbour. Two freshwater lakes, Dhadimagi Kilhi and Bandaara Kilhi, lie inland. At the northern tip, the beach of Thūndu is made of small, smooth white pebbles found nowhere else in the country.</p><p>The name joins fuvah, the local word for the areca nut, to Mulah, probably the island's original name. Admiralty charts called it Fua Mulaku and old French maps Poue Molluque. Its people speak a distinctive dialect of Dhivehi, known locally as Fuvahmulaki baha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The island's oldest remains date from before its conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The ruin of a Buddhist stupa, the Fua Mulaku Havitta, stands about 40 feet high, though its shape was altered after excavation, and Thor Heyerdahl visited the island and wrote a book about it, titled Fua Mulaku in its German edition. The Vasho-Veyo, a circular bath with stone steps, shows skilled cutting of Porites coral stone. Gemmiskiy, the oldest building, was part of a Buddhist monastery and became the first mosque after the islanders converted. The Hukuru Miskiy was long the only Friday mosque, and its cemetery holds princes of the Dhiyamigilee and Huraage dynasties.</p><p>Fuvahmulah has produced many scholars, among them the cleric and poet Mohammad Jamaaluddeen, known as Naibu-Thutthu, who wrote raivaru couplets and served as chief justice. Former President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-nasir">Ibrahim Nasir</a> was born on the island and spent his early childhood there before his family moved to Malé.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fuvahmulah forms the administrative atoll of Gnaviyani and has city status, with a council of seven members elected by its wards. The 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 9,166 residents. A hospital has served the island since 2001, and an airstrip on its south-eastern side has operated since 11 November 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fuvahmulah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent in the three southernmost atolls, Addu, Huvadhu and <b>Fuvahmulah</b>, over taxes and Male's attempts to restrict trade led to revolt in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-suvadive-republic">United Suvadive Republic</a></li></ul></section>
